      "writtenOn": "2026-10-02T20:21:50Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Should we also validate that the codec dimensions fit within its directory size?  The sort metric favors large codec dimensions, so we could end up with a first entry that fits within the canvas but exceeds its directory size - which I think would confuse Blink.\n\nE.g.\n```\nentry0 dirSize: 64x64, payloadSize: 32x32\nentry1 dirSize: 1x1, payloadSize: 48x48\n```\nSince entry1 passes `fitsCanvas` and has the largest payload size it gets promoted to frame 0, and I think trying to decode it using a 1x1 frame size would fail.",
      "revId": "f38ff95661c148762782f29060af3ff1f83f1d9a",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "5ce498d2_a50b64a0",
        "filename": "experimental/rust_ico/decoder/impl/SkIcoRustCodec.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 291,
      "author": {
        "id": 4159917
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-10-02T21:27:50Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Good catch. Checking only against the global canvas is too weak. I changed the fitting invariant to require each payload’s native dimensions to fit that entry’s directory-reported frame size, and reused it for sorting, scaled-size selection, exact matching, indexed decode, and canvas fallback. I also added a regression where a 128x128 payload is reported as 1x1 while a 64x64 payload is validly reported as 128x128; the valid entry now controls frame zero and the oversized one remains metadata-only.",
      "parentUuid": "eae12d6e_c20d3738",
      "revId": "f38ff95661c148762782f29060af3ff1f83f1d9a",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
